  "query_preview": "/WEDNESDAY THROUGH SUNDAY/...\nIssued at 353 AM CDT Mon Sep 28 2026\n\nThe surface low associated with former Hurricane Polo will track \nnorthward across New Mexico and into southeast Colorado by Wednesday \nmorning. This will be largely steered by closed upper low that \nshould be located across northwest New Mexico Wednesday morning. \nThis scenario continues to be supported by last several runs of the \noperational ECMWF and the ENS. Heavy rainfall will be associated \nwith this feature, with widespread amounts of 2\"+ across much of \neastern Colorado through the day Wednesday. The heavier rainfall has \nshifted northward into southern and southwest Nebraska. The latest \n28/00Z operational run of the ECMWF is projecting a band of 1-2\"+ of \nrainfall along I-80 and points south across southwest Nebraska. \nThis is supported by several of the Hi-Res tropical (hurricane \nmodels). The 24 hour ENS QPF mean now has a 30-50% chance of \n1\"+ across much southwest Nebraska along and to the south of \nI-80 through Wednesday. WPC is highlighting the areas from near \nSidney to Thedford to Ainsworth in a slight risk for excessive \nrainfall through the day Wednesday. The latest forecast trends \nwill have to be monitored, but it continues to look more likely \nthat heavy rainfall will occur across at least portions of \nsouthwest into southern Nebraska Wednesday into Wednesday night.\nWith this being moisture originating from the tropics, some \nlocally very heavy amounts greater than 3\" cannot be ruled out. \nThis will be especially true if the northward shift remains \nintact through the coming forecast cycles . \n\nBy Thursday, it appears that the northern stream flow will begin to \nsag southward and this will shunt the heavier rainfall south \nand east of the area. In addition, a cooler Canadian airmass \nwill sink southward into the region. Mainly dry weather is \nexpected Friday into the weekend with below normal temperatures.",
